<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">A Portuguesa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1890">1890</a>; national anthem since <a href="/years/1911">1911</a>; official version approved 16 July <a href="/years/1957">1957</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">On 11 January 1890 the United Kingdom issued an ultimatum demanding that Portugal give up colonising the land between its colonies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, on the west coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, on the east, which would have joined them into one territory, as proposed on the Rose-coloured Map. Despite a popular uproar, the Portuguese government accepted the demands.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…accepted, the composer Alfredo Keil, at the suggestion of a group of friends that included Rafael Bordalo Pinheiro and Teófilo Braga, wrote the melody of &quot;<b>A Portuguesa</b>&quot; as a patriotic protest march, and the writer Henrique Lopes de Mendonça agreed to Keil's request for words to fit it.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-portuguesa">A Portuguesa</a></li></ul></section>
